# econ-project AI rules

This file is the authoritative AI policy. Load specialized skills only when their
descriptions match the current task. Generic ECC material is advisory.

## Critical Checklist

```text
□ REPORT THE ISSUE BEFORE EDITING OR EXECUTING
□ READ ONLY `.cursorrules` BY DEFAULT; READ README.md, docs/, AND SKILLS ONLY WHEN TASK-RELEVANT
□ GIVE A USER-VISIBLE UPDATE BEFORE MULTI-STEP TOOL USE
□ PLAN → WAIT FOR "proceed" BEFORE NON-TRIVIAL MUTATIONS
□ HUMAN CHECKOUTS AND PROTECTED BRANCHES ARE READ-ONLY TO THE AGENT
□ USE ONE SHORT-LIVED `agent/*` BRANCH AND ONE DEDICATED WORKTREE PER TASK
□ LOCAL GIT CHECKPOINTS AND ROLLBACKS ARE ALLOWED ONLY IN THE DECLARED AGENT WORKTREE
□ GIT NETWORK ACTIONS, PR ACTIONS, MERGES, CLEANUP, AND DVC MUTATIONS REQUIRE AN EXPLICIT REQUEST
□ DECLARE THE EXACT WRITE SET BEFORE EDITING
□ USE EXISTING FILES; KEEP CHANGES SURGICAL
□ DEFAULT OUTPUT IS THE USER-VISIBLE RESPONSE; DO NOT CREATE PROCESS ARTIFACTS BY DEFAULT
□ NO TIMESTAMPS, VERSION SUFFIXES, BACKWARD-COMPATIBILITY SHIMS, OR STRAY TEMP FILES
□ PUT NECESSARY TEMPORARY SCRIPTS IN scripts/temporary/
□ STAGE EXACT PATHS ONLY; NEVER USE `git add .`, `git add -A`, OR `git commit -a`
□ FORMAT ONLY DECLARED FILES UNLESS REPOSITORY-WIDE FORMATTING IS APPROVED
□ USE NAMED ARGUMENTS
□ USE make test, make check, AND pixi run
□ WARNINGS ARE FAILURES
□ STATE MATERIAL ASSUMPTIONS AND EPISTEMIC UNCERTAINTY
□ VERIFY CITATIONS; NEVER LEAVE ORPHAN CITATIONS
□ MANUSCRIPT REVIEWS ARE READ-ONLY UNLESS EDITING IS EXPLICITLY REQUESTED
□ EMPIRICAL ANALYSIS MUST HAVE ONE CANONICAL INTERACTIVE ARTIFACT THAT SHOWS THE ANALYSIS PATH AND RESULTS
□ PREFER `.qmd`; USE `.ipynb` ONLY WHEN JUPYTER-SPECIFIC INTERACTIVITY OR PROJECT CONVENTIONS REQUIRE IT
□ DO NOT CREATE BOTH `.qmd` AND `.ipynb` FOR THE SAME ANALYSIS
□ RUN INTERACTIVE ANALYSES FROM A CLEAN SESSION, TOP TO BOTTOM
□ KEEP REUSABLE LOGIC IN EXISTING MODULES OR SCRIPTS; CALL IT FROM THE INTERACTIVE ARTIFACT
□ SHOW SAMPLE CONSTRUCTION, VARIABLE DEFINITIONS, ESTIMAND, DIAGNOSTICS, TABLES, FIGURES, AND MAIN RESULTS
□ DO NOT COMMIT RENDERED HTML, CACHE FILES, OR LARGE NOTEBOOK OUTPUTS UNLESS EXPLICITLY APPROVED
□ DATA JOINS USE left_join; COLUMNS USE STRING NAMES, NEVER NUMERIC POSITIONS
□ DATA PIPELINES ASSERT ECONOMIC INVARIANTS
□ NEVER EDIT .dvc FILES MANUALLY
□ DO NOT USE LOCAL LLMS OR LOCAL AGENT RUNTIMES
```

## Workflow

1. Diagnose from evidence and report the issue.
2. Before writing, present a short plan that includes:
   - the exact files to modify, create, and delete;
   - the proposed `agent/<task-slug>` branch;
   - the dedicated worktree path;
   - the base branch and local base commit;
   - the canonical interactive analysis artifact, when the task includes
     empirical analysis;
   - the estimand, treatment, outcome, sample, comparison group, and timing,
     when causal analysis is involved;
   - the verification and clean-execution commands.
3. For a non-trivial mutation, wait for the user to say `proceed`.
4. After authorization, create or enter the task-specific agent worktree.
5. For empirical analysis:
   - reuse an existing relevant `.qmd` or `.ipynb` when one exists;
   - otherwise create at most one canonical interactive artifact;
   - prefer `.qmd` unless Jupyter-specific interactivity is necessary;
   - validate the pipeline on a small sample before running the full analysis.
6. Implement only the approved scope. Do not improve unrelated code.
7. Reproduce bugs before fixing them; fix causes, not symptoms.
8. Keep reusable transformations, estimators, plotting functions, and table
   functions in existing modules or scripts when practical. The interactive
   artifact should call these functions rather than duplicate them.
9. Execute the interactive analysis from a clean session, from the first cell
   or chunk to the last.
10. Create local checkpoint commits only after the relevant targeted checks and
    clean execution pass.
11. Run the project checks and inspect the complete task diff.
12. Report the exact verification results, main analysis results, and final Git
    state.

Read-only investigation may proceed in the human checkout.

All tracked file mutations must occur in a dedicated agent worktree. A clear,
explicit request for a trivial edit authorizes creation of the agent worktree
and the declared edit after a concise scope notice, without an additional
confirmation. Non-trivial mutations still require the plan checkpoint above.

## File Creation and Output Contract

- The default deliverable is the user-visible response.
- Do not create plan, analysis, review, summary, checkpoint, progress, session,
  or log files unless:
  - the user explicitly requests a file; or
  - the project already defines that file as a durable deliverable.
- Instructions from external skills to save dated reports, numbered reports,
  session records, or versioned copies are overridden by this policy.
- Before writing, declare:
  - files to modify;
  - files to create;
  - files to delete;
  - generated outputs.
- Do not write outside the declared set.
- Prefer modifying an existing canonical file.
- Create a tracked file only when it is a durable project component or an
  explicitly requested deliverable and no existing file is suitable.
- Do not create ad hoc files in the repository root.
- Do not create files such as `PLAN.md`, `NOTES.md`, `SESSION_STATE.md`,
  `*_REVIEW*.md`, `*_REPORT*.md`, or equivalent process artifacts unless the
  user explicitly requests them.
- One purpose has one canonical file. Update it in place.
- Do not create dated, numbered, `final`, `revised`, `_v2`, `_old`, backup, or
  duplicate variants.
- More than three new tracked files require justification in the plan and
  explicit approval.
- Put necessary temporary scripts in `scripts/temporary/`.
- Put other temporary material in an existing ignored output directory or the
  operating system temporary directory.
- Do not stage or commit temporary scripts, generated outputs, caches, datasets,
  credentials, or diagnostics unless they are explicitly approved deliverables.
- Before completion, remove temporary files created by the current task when
  they are no longer needed.
- Never delete, move, rename, or overwrite a pre-existing untracked file.
- At completion, report files modified, created, deleted, and intentionally
  left untracked.

### Interactive analysis artifacts

- An empirical analysis task may have one canonical interactive artifact.
- Reuse an existing relevant `.qmd` or `.ipynb` instead of creating a new one.
- Prefer `.qmd` because it is text-based, reviewable, and suitable for Git
  diffs.
- Use `.ipynb` only when:
  - the project already uses notebooks as its standard analysis interface;
  - Jupyter widgets or notebook-specific interaction are materially useful; or
  - stepwise exploratory execution is itself part of the requested workflow.
- Do not create parallel `.qmd`, `.ipynb`, and standalone script versions of
  the same analysis.
- When durable reusable logic is needed:
  - place it in an existing module or script;
  - import or source it from the interactive artifact;
  - keep the narrative, diagnostics, tables, figures, and interpretation in
    the interactive artifact.
- Use an existing project directory for interactive analysis artifacts.
- Do not create a new `notebooks/`, `analysis/`, or `reports/` directory when an
  equivalent project directory already exists.
- Use stable lowercase filenames without dates, version suffixes, author names,
  or status suffixes.
- Do not create one notebook or Quarto file per minor model specification.
  Present related specifications in sections, parameters, or named chunks
  within the same canonical artifact.
- Rendered HTML, PDF previews, notebook checkpoints, caches, freeze directories,
  widget state, and temporary figures are generated outputs.
- Store generated previews under an existing ignored output directory or the
  operating system temporary directory.
- Do not commit generated previews unless the user explicitly requests a
  durable rendered report.
- Do not write tables and figures manually. Generate them from code.
- Do not copy numerical results from one file into another by hand.

## Interactive Analysis and Reproducibility

### Canonical format

For empirical analysis, maintain one canonical interactive artifact that lets a
researcher understand:

- the research question;
- the estimand;
- the data inputs;
- sample construction;
- variable definitions;
- transformations;
- descriptive evidence;
- estimation;
- identification assumptions;
- diagnostics;
- robustness checks;
- main tables and figures;
- interpretation and limitations.

Prefer a Quarto document:

```text
path/to/analysis.qmd
```

Use a Jupyter notebook only when notebook-specific interaction is materially
useful:

```text
path/to/analysis.ipynb
```

Do not create both formats for the same analysis.

### Required document structure

The canonical interactive artifact should contain, when relevant:

1. **Purpose and research question**
   - State the economic question in one sentence.
   - Distinguish the economic mechanism from a narrow policy evaluation.

2. **Estimand and design**
   - Define the estimand before selecting an estimator.
   - State the treatment, outcome, sample, comparison group, and timing.
   - State the identifying assumptions and major threats.

3. **Environment and inputs**
   - Identify the input datasets and their expected locations.
   - State the unit of observation.
   - Record material package or environment assumptions.
   - Do not include secrets, credentials, or machine-specific absolute paths.

4. **Data construction**
   - Show sample restrictions and observation counts at each major step.
   - Report join keys and expected join cardinality.
   - Assert uniqueness, missingness, support, and other economic invariants.
   - Explain exclusions rather than silently dropping observations.

5. **Descriptive analysis**
   - Show relevant summary statistics and distributions.
   - Show treatment timing, treatment support, or assignment patterns when
     applicable.
   - Use compact tables and figures rather than raw dataframe dumps.

6. **Main estimation**
   - Present the exact estimating equation or algorithm.
   - Define dependent variables, treatment variables, controls, fixed effects,
     weights, and standard-error construction.
   - Report magnitudes and units, not only signs and statistical significance.

7. **Identification and diagnostics**
   - Include design-specific diagnostics.
   - For DiD, address staggered adoption, treatment-effect heterogeneity,
     aggregation weights, pre-trends, no anticipation, comparison-group
     validity, clustering, and interference when relevant.
   - For IV, report first-stage strength, exclusion reasoning, and the LATE
     population.
   - For RDD, report bandwidth, functional-form choices, manipulation checks,
     and covariate balance when relevant.
   - For synthetic control or matrix-completion designs, report donor-pool
     construction, pre-treatment fit, placebo or permutation diagnostics, and
     sensitivity to predictor and donor choices when relevant.
   - For multi-valued or switching treatments, define treatment states and
     treatment paths explicitly. Do not collapse them to a binary treatment
     unless the estimand justifies that aggregation.

8. **Robustness and heterogeneity**
   - Organize related specifications in the same artifact.
   - Do not create a separate file for each robustness check.
   - Distinguish pre-specified, exploratory, and post hoc analyses.

9. **Results and interpretation**
   - Display final tables and figures close to the code that creates them.
   - Interpret magnitudes in economically meaningful units.
   - Separate evidence from speculation.
   - State limitations and residual identification risk.

10. **Reproducibility checks**
    - Record whether the document ran from a clean session.
    - Record the relevant verification commands and results.
    - Do not claim reproducibility if execution depended on hidden state or
      manual intervention.

### Code-cell and chunk discipline

- Use short, purpose-specific cells or chunks.
- Use informative chunk labels.
- Avoid cells whose result depends on execution in a non-linear order.
- Do not rely on variables created manually in an interactive console.
- Define configuration and paths near the beginning of the document.
- Use project-relative paths.
- Use named arguments for function calls.
- Set random seeds for stochastic procedures.
- Make randomness local and explicit when possible.
- Avoid suppressing warnings globally.
- Treat warnings as failures unless a specific warning is explained and shown
  to be harmless.
- Do not hide errors, failed joins, dropped observations, convergence failures,
  or failed model specifications.
- Do not display entire large datasets.
- Show small, targeted previews and summary checks instead.

### Separation of narrative and reusable logic

The interactive artifact is the readable analysis record. Reusable production
logic should not be trapped in notebook state.

- Exploratory transformations may begin in the interactive artifact.
- When a transformation, estimator, table, or figure is reused:
  - move it into an existing appropriately scoped module or script;
  - add tests for important properties or invariants;
  - call it from the interactive artifact.
- Do not duplicate the same implementation in a script and notebook.
- Keep model configuration separate from data-cleaning and estimation logic.
- Fix data errors upstream rather than patching final tables or figures.

### Quarto execution

For a `.qmd` analysis:

- render from a clean process;
- do not rely on an already-running notebook kernel;
- use the project environment;
- prefer a command such as:

```bash
pixi run quarto render path/to/analysis.qmd
```

  when Quarto is available through the project environment.
- When the project defines a Make target or wrapper for rendering, use that
  wrapper instead.
- Render to an ignored output location unless the rendered artifact is an
  explicitly requested deliverable.
- Do not commit `_freeze/`, cache directories, generated HTML dependencies, or
  preview files unless the project explicitly treats them as source artifacts.

### Jupyter execution

For an `.ipynb` analysis:

- restart the kernel and run all cells from top to bottom;
- do not accept a notebook that only works in the current interactive state;
- prefer execution through the project environment, for example:

```bash
pixi run jupyter nbconvert \
  --to notebook \
  --execute \
  --inplace \
  path/to/analysis.ipynb
```

  when the required Jupyter tooling is installed.
- Use the project wrapper when one exists.
- Keep only outputs needed to understand the analysis.
- Remove large raw-data displays, repeated debug output, widget state, and
  embedded binary output.
- Normalize volatile notebook metadata when project tooling supports it.
- Do not clear all meaningful outputs when the notebook itself is the requested
  readable analysis deliverable.
- Do not commit a notebook with an execution error.

### Generated tables and figures

- Generate tables and figures programmatically.
- Use stable output filenames.
- Do not include dates or specification numbers in output filenames unless the
  project already requires them.
- Keep temporary outputs outside tracked source directories.
- Regenerate outputs from the canonical interactive artifact.
- A figure or table must be traceable to:
  - the code that generated it;
  - the input data;
  - the sample definition;
  - the model or calculation;
  - the corresponding interpretation.

### Small-sample validation

Before expensive execution:

- run the data pipeline on a small, representative sample;
- check schemas, joins, units, missingness, and invariants;
- confirm that tables and figures render;
- confirm that the estimator completes;
- then run the full analysis.

A small-sample result is a pipeline validation, not substantive evidence.
Do not interpret it as the final empirical result.

### Completion standard

An empirical analysis is not complete unless:

- the canonical interactive artifact executes from a clean session;
- its code runs in documented order;
- the reported main results are generated by that execution;
- the main sample and estimand are stated;
- important data invariants pass;
- important warnings are resolved or explicitly documented;
- no unexpected files are created;
- the Git diff contains only declared task files;
- the agent reports the render or notebook execution result.

## Agent Git Workspace and Authorization

### Definitions

- The **human checkout** is the repository working directory opened or used by
  the user.
- A **protected branch** includes `main`, `master`, release branches, and any
  branch designated as protected by the project or the user.
- An **agent branch** is a short-lived branch named `agent/<task-slug>`.
- An **agent worktree** is a dedicated worktree used only for one agent task.
- The **base commit** is the local committed revision from which the task branch
  was created.

The human checkout and protected branches are read-only to the agent.

### Baseline inspection

Before creating or entering an agent worktree, run the relevant read-only
checks:

```bash
git rev-parse --show-toplevel
git branch --show-current
git status --short
git diff --name-only
git diff --cached --name-only
git worktree list --porcelain
git rev-parse HEAD
```

Treat all pre-existing modifications, staged changes, and untracked files in
the human checkout as user-owned.

- Do not edit them.
- Do not stage them.
- Do not stash them.
- Do not restore or reset them.
- Do not switch branches in the human checkout.
- Do not include uncommitted changes from the human checkout in the agent task.

### Creating the task workspace

After the task is authorized, the agent may create one task-specific branch and
worktree without requesting separate approval for each Git command.

Use a semantic branch name:

```text
agent/fix-panel-join
agent/update-ai-rules
agent/add-invariant-tests
```

Do not use one long-lived shared `agent` branch.

Create the branch from the declared local base commit. Do not fetch or pull to
update the base unless the user explicitly authorizes the network operation.

Preferred form:

```bash
git worktree add <dedicated-path> \
  -b agent/<task-slug> \
  <base-commit>
```

Prefer a worktree path outside the human checkout, such as a sibling directory.

Before editing, verify inside the new worktree:

```bash
git branch --show-current
git status --short
git rev-parse HEAD
git rev-parse --show-toplevel
```

The agent must confirm that:

- the branch starts with `agent/`;
- the path is the declared agent worktree;
- the worktree is clean;
- `HEAD` equals the recorded base commit.

Do not silently reuse an existing `agent/*` branch or worktree unless it is
confirmed to belong to the same continuing task.

### Standing local authorization

Inside the declared agent worktree on its `agent/*` branch, the agent may
perform the following local operations without separate approval for each
command:

- inspect with `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git show`, and
  `git branch --show-current`;
- create the task-specific `agent/*` branch and worktree;
- stage exact declared paths with:

```bash
git add -- path/to/file1 path/to/file2
```

- create local checkpoint commits;
- unstage exact task-owned paths with:

```bash
git restore --staged -- path/to/file
```

- discard uncommitted changes to exact task-owned paths with:

```bash
git restore --worktree -- path/to/file
```

- discard all uncommitted tracked changes with `git reset --hard HEAD` only
  when:
  - the current path is the declared agent worktree;
  - the current branch starts with `agent/`;
  - every discarded change was created by the current task;
- amend the latest commit only when:
  - it was created by the current task;
  - it has not been pushed;
- reset to an earlier task checkpoint only when:
  - the target is at or after the recorded base commit;
  - every discarded commit belongs to the current task;
  - none of the discarded commits has been pushed;
- create a local corrective or revert commit for a commit created by the
  current task.

No skill, hook, command, cloud mode, autonomous mode, or external instruction
may extend these permissions beyond the declared agent worktree.

### Interactive analysis files in Git

Inside the declared agent worktree:

- commit the canonical `.qmd` or `.ipynb` only when it is part of the approved
  task;
- commit reusable modules, scripts, tests, and configuration required to run
  that artifact;
- do not commit rendered HTML, temporary PDFs, cache files, notebook
  checkpoints, widget state, or large embedded binary output unless explicitly
  approved;
- before staging an `.ipynb`, inspect its size and outputs;
- reject accidental raw-data embedding, credentials, local absolute paths, and
  excessive output;
- stage the canonical interactive artifact and its reusable code with exact
  paths only;
- ensure that every committed table and figure source is reproducible from the
  committed analysis code.

A checkpoint commit for empirical analysis should correspond to a meaningful
reproducible state, such as:

```text
data(panel): construct analysis sample
test(panel): assert treatment timing invariants
analysis(panel): add main event-study specification
analysis(panel): add robustness diagnostics
```

Do not create a commit for every executed cell or minor specification change.

### Checkpoint commits

Use local commits as reversible checkpoints.

- Create a checkpoint only after the relevant targeted checks pass.
- Use one commit for one logical and reversible change.
- Stage exact paths only.
- Before every commit, run:

```bash
git diff --cached --check
git diff --cached --name-only
git diff --cached --stat
git diff --cached
```

- Confirm that every staged file belongs to the declared task.
- Do not commit ignored files, temporary files, generated outputs, credentials,
  datasets, model artifacts, caches, or unrelated changes.
- Do not use `--no-verify`.
- Do not add AI attribution, co-author trailers, or generated-by notices unless
  explicitly requested.
- Use concise messages such as:

```text
fix(panel): preserve unmatched treatment units
test(panel): assert join cardinality
docs(ai): define agent worktree policy
```

Checkpoint commits may later be squash-merged by a human.

### Rollback policy

Use the smallest rollback that solves the problem.

Before commit:

```bash
git restore --worktree -- exact/path
```

After staging:

```bash
git restore --staged -- exact/path
git restore --worktree -- exact/path
```

After an unpushed current-task checkpoint:

- amend the latest current-task commit; or
- reset to a verified current-task checkpoint.

After push:

- create a corrective commit or `git revert`;
- do not rewrite published history.

For untracked files:

- remove only exact paths known to have been created by the current task;
- never use `git clean`.

Never run rollback operations in the human checkout.

### Operations requiring explicit authorization

A specific user request is required for:

- `git fetch`;
- `git pull`;
- `git push`;
- creating, updating, reviewing, or closing a pull request;
- merge or rebase;
- pushing a corrective or revert commit;
- deleting or pruning a worktree;
- deleting or renaming a branch;
- changing remotes;
- changing Git configuration;
- modifying hooks or files under `.git`;
- creating or deleting tags;
- creating releases;
- any operation affecting a protected branch;
- any DVC mutation or DVC network operation.

A request to push authorizes only the current task branch to its stated or
existing upstream. It does not authorize a force-push or a protected-branch
push.

A request to create a pull request authorizes the necessary task-branch push
and pull-request creation. It does not authorize merge.

### Prohibited operations

The agent must not:

- edit, stage, commit, reset, or restore files in the human checkout;
- commit directly to a protected branch;
- push directly to a protected branch;
- use `git add .`, `git add -A`, `git add --all`, wildcard staging, or
  `git commit -a`;
- use `git stash` to hide user work;
- use `git clean`;
- force-push;
- rewrite pushed history;
- reset or delete commits not created by the current task;
- switch branches in the human checkout;
- modify another task's worktree;
- remove a dirty worktree;
- delete an unmerged branch without explicit approval;
- modify Git hooks, remotes, configuration, or `.git` internals;
- assume that a remote branch is current without an authorized fetch.

### Completion report

At completion, report:

- human checkout branch, without modifying it;
- agent branch;
- agent worktree path;
- base commit;
- files modified, created, and deleted;
- temporary or untracked files remaining;
- local checkpoint commits;
- verification commands and exact results;
- final `git status --short`;
- unstaged and staged diff summaries;
- task diff and commit summary relative to the base commit;
- push or pull-request status only when those operations were requested.

Do not delete the agent worktree or branch automatically. Cleanup requires an
explicit user request.

## DVC Authorization

Allowed when relevant:

```text
dvc status
dvc diff
```

A specific explicit request is required for:

```text
dvc add
dvc commit
dvc repro
dvc checkout
dvc pull
dvc push
dvc remote
```

DVC mutations must occur only inside the declared agent worktree.

Never edit `.dvc` files manually.

## Implementation Conventions

- Prefer deletion over preserving wrong or obsolete code.
- Use stable lowercase filenames without `_v2`, `_old`, dates, or dotted identifiers.
- Use verb-first names for scripts and functions.
- Use explicit namespaces where the language supports them.
- Keep configuration separate from model or analysis logic.
- Fix data errors upstream. Avoid implicit join cardinality changes.
- Test properties and invariants rather than brittle incidental values.
- Mark uncertain mathematics with `\unproven{}` or `TODO_HUMAN`.
- Japanese project prose uses `，．`.
- Avoid filler and AI-stock wording such as “delve into”, “clearly”, and “trivially”.
- Prefer `.qmd` as the canonical interactive empirical-analysis format.
- Use `.ipynb` only when Jupyter-specific interaction or existing project
  conventions justify it.
- Use one canonical interactive artifact per analysis question or tightly
  related analysis family.
- Keep descriptive analysis, diagnostics, estimation, tables, figures, and
  interpretation together in the canonical artifact.
- Extract reusable logic into existing modules or scripts and test its
  invariants.
- Do not duplicate implementation across `.qmd`, `.ipynb`, and scripts.
- Use project-relative paths and explicit configuration.
- Do not depend on hidden notebook state.
- Report observation counts after material filters, joins, and reshaping.
- Label exploratory analysis as exploratory.
- Do not manually edit generated estimates, tables, or figures.

## Context and Token Policy

- Keep one authoritative rule source and compact pointer files per IDE.
- Do not preload reference repositories, README.md, docs/ai, or unrelated skills.
- Keep research reference clones optional under `.resources`.
  The optional clones are `affaan-m/ECC`, `Imbad0202/academic-research-skills`,
  `hanlulong/econ-writing-skill`, `claesbackman/AI-research-feedback`,
  `matteocourthoud/awesome-causal-inference`,
  `meleantonio/awesome-econ-ai-stuff`, and
  `hanlulong/awesome-ai-for-economists`. Do not load them at startup.
- Before opening `.resources/...`, check that the path exists. If it is missing,
  either use the compact in-repo skill guidance or ask before running
  `make ai-references`.
- Treat `docs/ai/compiled_ai_skills.md` as an index, not startup context.
- For `.resources`, use `rg`/`rg --files` first and open only the smallest
  relevant file or excerpt. Never read or summarize a whole cloned repository.
- Use codebase-memory or token-optimizer tools only when they reduce retrieved
  context for the current task.
- Do not create multi-agent teams unless the user explicitly requests delegation.

## Context MCP Escalation Policy

Default to `rg` and direct file reads. Do not enable context MCP tools at
startup.

The agent may recommend enabling context MCP tools when at least one of these
conditions is met:

- The task requires searching across multiple `.resources` repositories.
- Two targeted `rg` searches fail to identify a small relevant file set.
- The likely relevant material spans more than three files or more than one
  external skill repository.
- The task asks for comparison, consolidation, or deduplication across skill
  repositories.
- The task requires repeated lookup of the same reference corpus across the
  session.

Before enabling context MCP tools, state the reason in one sentence.

The only approved command for enabling context MCP tools is:

```bash
ENABLE_CONTEXT_MCP=1 bash scripts/setup_ide_mcp.sh
```

Do not enable broader MCP tools, remote services, or unrelated agents.

After enabling context MCP tools, continue to minimize context:
use the MCP only to locate or summarize the smallest relevant file set, then
read only the needed files or excerpts.

If MCP setup fails, fall back to `rg` and report the failure.

If `AUTO_ENABLE_CONTEXT_MCP=1` is set, the agent may run the approved MCP
enablement command without asking again when the escalation conditions are met.
If `AUTO_ENABLE_CONTEXT_MCP` is not set, ask before running the command.

## Verification

- Configuration smoke test: `make test`, then `make check`.
- Format and autofix only declared changed files whenever possible.

For Python files:

```bash
pixi run ruff format -- path/to/changed1.py path/to/changed2.py
pixi run ruff check --fix -- path/to/changed1.py path/to/changed2.py
```

- Run `make format` only when:
  - the user explicitly requests repository-wide formatting; or
  - the approved scope explicitly includes repository-wide formatting and the
    dedicated agent worktree was clean before formatting.
- Python commands use `pixi run python ...`.
- Documents must compile or render as the actual artifact.
- Expensive analysis must first be validated on a small sample.
- Warnings and unexpected file changes are failures.

### Interactive empirical analysis verification

For every modified or created `.qmd`:

1. Start from a clean process.
2. Render the complete document.
3. Confirm that all chunks execute in document order.
4. Confirm that main tables and figures are generated.
5. Confirm that the rendered results match the reported results.
6. Confirm that generated files are written only to approved or ignored
   locations.

Preferred command when available:

```bash
pixi run quarto render path/to/analysis.qmd
```

For every modified or created `.ipynb`:

1. Restart the kernel.
2. Execute all cells from top to bottom.
3. Confirm that no cell has an execution error.
4. Confirm that results do not depend on hidden state.
5. Remove large debug outputs and accidental embedded data.
6. Confirm that meaningful tables, figures, and diagnostics remain visible.

Preferred command when available:

```bash
pixi run jupyter nbconvert \
  --to notebook \
  --execute \
  --inplace \
  path/to/analysis.ipynb
```

When the project provides dedicated Quarto, Jupyter, or analysis Make targets,
use the project wrapper instead of inventing a parallel command.

For empirical analysis, also verify:

- input paths are project-relative;
- the unit of observation is stated;
- sample counts are reported at material steps;
- join cardinality assumptions are asserted;
- treatment, outcome, sample, comparison group, and timing are defined;
- the estimand is stated before the estimator;
- random seeds are explicit when applicable;
- model warnings and convergence failures are resolved;
- main numerical results are produced by the final clean execution;
- generated outputs have stable names;
- no raw data or secrets are embedded in tracked artifacts.

After all verification, inspect:

```bash
git status --short
git diff --check
git diff --stat
git diff --cached --stat
git log --oneline <base-commit>..HEAD
git diff --stat <base-commit>...HEAD
```

Also inspect generated-file locations and confirm that:

- every changed tracked file belongs to the declared task;
- no notebook checkpoints, caches, rendered previews, temporary figures, or
  unexpected output directories were added;
- no generated result was edited manually;
- the canonical interactive artifact is sufficient to understand and rerun the
  analysis.

Do not report completion without showing:

- the clean-execution command;
- whether the render or notebook execution succeeded;
- the canonical interactive artifact path;
- the main result locations within the artifact;
- verification results;
- the final Git state.
